Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Evaluating Black-Box Physics Through Optical Emulation", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"18b3b19f-7fbe-fe4a-b751-4dcbda815f94"],

Cell["An illustrated computational essay, in twelve stages", "Subtitle",ExpressionUUID->"5a7949f5-96cd-e543-ac5f-a1b0486a9dd0"],

Cell["Hubert Ko\[LSlash]cz", "Author",ExpressionUUID->"d80a993b-2bd0-454f-a5f5-227e2ae6224e"],

Cell["\<\
Given only black-box access to a device \[LongDash] the settings that go in, \
the clicks that come out \[LongDash] under what conditions is it \
mathematically impossible to distinguish a genuinely quantum machine from an \
efficient classical optical emulation of it? This essay answers by building \
the certification out of two provably independent lenses: a correlation lens \
that classifies measurement statistics by graph invariants, and a geometric \
lens that prices resource scaling by a dynamical Lie algebra. The KCBS \
pentagon is the atom, and its exact hierarchy \[LongDash] classical 2 < \
quantum Sqrt[5] < exclusivity-only 5/2 \[LongDash] reappears at every stage; \
pentagon-mesh gluing supplies the composition algebra; analogue Hawking \
radiation supplies the application. The essay is built to be run: every \
number below is recomputed in this notebook's own kernel, and every stage \
carries at least one figure you can regenerate and drag. It proceeds start to \
finish \[LongDash] from the sealed box, through the smallest example and its \
one designed-in blind spot, the second lens that closes it, an optimal gluing \
pattern certified by ergodic optimization, and finally to two extensions the \
framework was not built for \[LongDash] ending on a single pentagon that \
catalyzes a clique of size 17 in a structure that would otherwise stay \
dormant. Established results are cited by number and used as tools; what \
follows is what they were used to build.\
\>", "Abstract",ExpressionUUID->"bbeeeaf6-796c-6c4a-91b4-0d8ce4be3f84"],

Cell["\<\
How to read this essay: it is meant to be evaluated. Choose Evaluation \
\[RightArrow] Evaluate Notebook to render every figure. The Wolfram Language \
code that generates the figures is collapsed by default \[LongDash] click a \
closed cell's bracket on the right (or select it and press the down-arrow \
key) to open, inspect, or edit it. Every quantity is recomputed live, so \
nothing here is a static image.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"959df006-c5d1-584a-91d4-bd75e1fd353e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Instrument: Two Lenses and Four Kinds of Check", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"fc5d6cd9-5ccc-1b49-8fea-3f7069d6fe2e"],

Cell["\<\
Everything below runs on one instrument, and it helps to see its shape before \
the story starts. Two lenses look at the box. The correlation lens draws a \
graph \[LongDash] measurements that can never both succeed are joined by an \
edge \[LongDash] and reads three invariants off it [1]. The geometric lens \
ignores the statistics entirely and instead counts how many independent \
motions a classical compiler would need to reproduce the device [2]. A box is \
safely classical-emulable only when both lenses agree; the interesting cases \
are exactly where one lens runs out of reach.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"29d2960a-fa52-2545-ba26-7db2e087682a"],

Cell["\<\
The checks themselves fall into four kinds, and it is worth keeping them \
straight, because only the first kind actually grades the device \[LongDash] \
the rest increasingly audit the measuring apparatus itself. This diagram is \
the map every later stage points back to:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"95e1ba6d-72b7-fc4e-a35a-cf938ab21d80"],

Cell["\<\
The assessment instrument at a glance, colour-coded by what each check \
interrogates:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"d6eebdea-2549-1740-9ee8-4befe6dbcee7"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{chip, cat, blue = RGBColor[0.20, 0.45, 0.85],
   green = RGBColor[0.30, 0.60, 0.35], amber = RGBColor[0.85, 0.55, 0.20],
   plum = RGBColor[0.62, 0.30, 0.55]},
 chip = Function[{t, c}, Framed[Style[t, 9, White, FontFamily -> \
\"Helvetica\"],
    Background -> c, FrameStyle -> White, RoundingRadius -> 4,
    ImageSize -> {172, 26}, Alignment -> Center]];
 cat = Function[{title, sub, chips, c}, Framed[Column[
     Prepend[chips, Column[{Style[title, Bold, 10, c],
        Style[sub, Italic, 8, GrayLevel[0.45]]}, Spacings -> 0.1]],
     Spacings -> 0.45, Alignment -> Center], FrameStyle -> c,
    RoundingRadius -> 8, Background -> Lighter[c, 0.9], ImageMargins -> 3]];
 Grid[{{
    cat[\"1 \[Bullet] DEVICE METRICS\", \"compare data \[Rule] fixed boundary\
\",
     {chip[\"C1 no-disturbance\", blue], chip[\"C2 contextual fraction\", \
blue],
      chip[\"C3 CE^2 clique load\", blue], chip[\"C4 possibilistic support\", \
blue],
      chip[\"C5 node sum vs 2, \[Sqrt]5, 5/2\", blue]}, blue],
    cat[\"2 \[Bullet] SELF-VALIDATION\", \"checks the tool, not a device\",
     {chip[\"sanity anchors\", green], chip[\"A1\[Dash]A5 blueprint audit\", \
green]}, green],
    cat[\"3 \[Bullet] TRUST / SCOPE\", \"what the engine cannot see\",
     {chip[\"G7 DLA hook (white-box)\", amber],
      chip[\"\[Eta]* = 2/\[Sqrt]5 reach theorem\", amber]}, amber],
    cat[\"4 \[Bullet] ACCESS EXPANSION\", \"re-engineer the measurement\",
     {chip[\"G8 attenuation (OQ2)\", plum], chip[\"G9 antibunching g^2(0)\", \
plum],
      chip[\"OQ1 interventional DLA\", plum]}, plum]
   }}, Spacings -> {0.6, 0}, Alignment -> Top]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[735]:=",ExpressionUUID->"30371c86-f7f3-1142-96f4-74eea37db6ba"],

