Cell["\<\
At the exact angle every adjacent pair is perpendicular \[LongDash] all five \
bars sit at zero \[LongDash] and the pentagon's fivefold symmetry forbids a \
partial failure, so moving the slider shifts them together. This is the \
object gate C5 is calibrated on. Its exact, algebraic invariants are what let \
every later stage run in certified arithmetic rather than by approximation.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"00a1585c-11fa-2f4f-a27c-b92cf254141d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Stage 2 \[LongDash] The Three-Number Ruler: 2 < \[Sqrt]5 < 5/2", \
"Section",ExpressionUUID->"3cc8b89e-771b-674c-9305-2009cac80c21"],

Cell["\<\
For this pentagon three exact numbers matter. Two is the best any classical \
device can score. Sqrt[5] \[TildeEqual] 2.236 is the best any genuine quantum \
device \[LongDash] a single photon \[LongDash] can score. And 5/2 is a \
theoretical ceiling that is mathematically possible under exclusivity alone \
but is not reachable by a real quantum particle. There is a quantum zone \
strictly between classical and this outer ceiling, and the gap Sqrt[5]-2 is \
the resource the whole project studies. The cleanest way to see all three at \
once is to read one pentagon three ways.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"928cae70-7a94-b943-b4b7-30e7541a5c6b"],

Cell["\<\
One pentagon, three readings of the same graph \[LongDash] the project's \
entire ruler on one line:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"a8688b8d-7038-b849-95bc-efc725e52a60"],

Cell["\<\
Module[{pts, pent},
 pts = Table[{Cos[Pi/2 + 2 Pi q/5], Sin[Pi/2 + 2 Pi q/5]}, {q, 0, 4}];
 pent = Function[{vals, cols, sVal, title, sub}, Labeled[
    Graphics[{
      {GrayLevel[0.65], Thickness[0.006], Line[Append[pts, First[pts]]]},
      Table[{cols[[q]], EdgeForm[GrayLevel[0.35]], Disk[pts[[q]], 0.2],
         If[MatchQ[cols[[q]], GrayLevel[_]], Black, White],
         Text[Style[vals[[q]], 10, Bold], pts[[q]]]}, {q, 1, 5}]},
     ImageSize -> 200, PlotRange -> 1.45, PlotRangePadding -> 0.12],
    Column[{Style[title, Bold, 10], Style[sub, Italic, 8, GrayLevel[0.45]],
       Style[\"S = \" <> sVal, 13, Bold]}, Alignment -> Center, Spacings -> \
0.25],
    Top]];
 Row[{
   pent[{\"1\", \"0\", \"1\", \"0\", \"0\"},
    {RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], GrayLevel[.82], RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], \
GrayLevel[.82], GrayLevel[.82]},
    \"2\", \"CLASSICAL\", \"one possible world\"],
   pent[{\".45\", \".45\", \".45\", \".45\", \".45\"},
    ConstantArray[RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], 5],
    \"\[Sqrt]5 \[TildeEqual] 2.236\", \"QUANTUM\", \"geometry of cos^2\"],
   pent[{\"\:00bd\", \"\:00bd\", \"\:00bd\", \"\:00bd\", \
\"\:00bd\"},
    ConstantArray[RGBColor[.85, .55, .2], 5],
    \"5/2\", \"EXCLUSIVITY ONLY\", \"Wright's one-half\"]
   }, Spacer[8]]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[741]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9e778c83-96d4-1045-8d9b-2ca89cedb34e"],

