Cell["\<\
Run against a genuine quantum sampler, a classical hidden-variable sampler, \
and a classical intensity emulator, the protocol works as hoped: the quantum \
device certifies within a few thousand clicks per context, and a naively \
built classical emulator is caught immediately. But notice what the shrinking \
interval does and does not do. It quickly separates the device from classical \
2 \[LongDash] so the box certifies \[LongDash] yet it never has to resolve \
Sqrt[5] from the 5/2 ceiling, because a device parked exactly at Sqrt[5] is, \
by construction, consistent with every Category-1 threshold. That residual \
silence is where the one blind spot lives.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"261b82dc-8323-b340-ad2f-da8accf3aa25"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Stage 4 \[LongDash] The Catch: a Classical Cheater Can Fake It \
Exactly", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"633c4ab9-f8d8-f847-9034-046b3d336209"],

Cell["\<\
Here is the twist. A classical light source, if it is allowed to report \
continuous brightness fractions instead of discrete particle clicks, can be \
tuned so that it produces exactly the same numbers as the real quantum device \
\[LongDash] not approximately, but to the last digit. Tuned to transmission t \
= 1/Sqrt[5], the intensity emulator reproduces the quantum table cell for \
cell, so at that one tuning the two devices' input-output statistics are \
identical and no amount of further statistics can separate them. This is a \
theorem, not a failure of effort, and it is an established experimental fact \
that suitably tuned classical fields reach Sqrt[5] on scenarios of exactly \
this kind [20, 26].\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2b161c7d-9e58-1e40-846c-f0587d9b44e3"],

Cell["\<\
Two different physical devices, one identical output \[LongDash] and why no \
test on the numbers can separate them:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"5847f1d3-07a4-7b4f-8237-abe6a4f77af3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Module[{},
 Graphics[{
   {RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Disk[{-3.5, 1}, 0.24]}, White, Text[Style[\"\
\[Gamma]\", 12], {-3.5, 1}],
   Black, Text[Style[\"quantum photon\", 8], {-3.5, 0.5}],
   {RGBColor[.85, .55, .2], Thickness[0.02], Line[{{-3.75, -0.8}, {-3.25, \
-1.2}}], Line[{{-3.75, -1.2}, {-3.25, -0.8}}]},
   Black, Text[Style[\"classical optics\", 8], {-3.5, -1.5}],
   Arrowheads[0.028], GrayLevel[0.4],
   Arrow[{{-3.0, 0.9}, {-1.25, 0.25}}], Arrow[{{-3.0, -0.9}, {-1.25, -0.25}}],
   {GrayLevel[0.96], EdgeForm[GrayLevel[0.5]], Rectangle[{-1.15, -0.62}, \
{1.35, 0.62}]},
   Black, Text[Style[\"one identical output table\", 9, Bold], {0.1, 0.35}],
   Text[Style[\"S = \[Sqrt]5,   CF = 2\[Sqrt]5-4 = 0.472\", 9], {0.1, 0.02}],
   RGBColor[.6, .3, .1], Text[Style[\"every C1\[Dash]C5 gate: PASS\", 8], \
{0.1, -0.34}],
   GrayLevel[0.4], Arrow[{{1.45, 0}, {2.4, 0}}],
   RGBColor[.8, .25, .15], Text[Style[\"\[Times]\", 26], {3.05, 0.12}],
   Black, Text[Style[\"no statistical test\", 8], {3.95, 0.08}], \
Text[Style[\"can tell them apart\", 8], {3.95, -0.15}],
   RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Text[Style[\"the blind spot: identical statistics, \
different physics \[LongDash] only the geometric lens (Stage 5) separates \
them\", 9, Bold], {-0.35, -2.05}]
   }, ImageSize -> 600, PlotRange -> {{-4.4, 5.2}, {-2.4, 1.6}}, ImagePadding \
-> 6]]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[746]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6560c66d-fafd-9445-a511-750135ed718c"],

