Cell["\<\
Both devices produce exactly the same output table, both node sums equal \
Sqrt[5], and both contextual fractions equal 2 Sqrt[5] - 4. Every Category-1 \
gate passes \[LongDash] which means the emulator earns a QUANTUM-CERTIFIED \
verdict it does not deserve. This is the framework's one designed-in blind \
spot, and it is the whole reason a second, completely different kind of \
evidence is needed. Statistics have run out; geometry has not.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"733f8698-8cca-de48-9c82-eadae6bfc08e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Stage 5 \[LongDash] The Second Lens: How Twisted Is the Motion?", \
"Section",ExpressionUUID->"3cb08de2-f6e6-0841-9cc3-725884c26618"],

Cell["\<\
The second check does not look at output statistics at all. It looks at the \
shape of the internal transformations the device claims to perform, using the \
dynamical Lie algebra [2]: how many independent motions must a classical \
compiler chain together to reproduce it? A genuine quantum pentagon needs its \
internal steps to twist in a fully three-dimensional way \[LongDash] the \
algebra dimension must reach 3. A classical light-splitting fake manages only \
a flatter, lower-dimensional twist. Re-derive the pentagon's own value \
directly: first the span the raw steps explore, then the closure after one \
round of composition.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e80669f8-a82b-6843-b19b-560798bbee44"],

Cell["\<\
The pentagon's dynamical Lie algebra: raw span, then commutator closure:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"91512737-5e73-d94d-b219-ffd724b498d9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
c2 = Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5]);
vecs = Table[{Sqrt[1 - c2] Cos[4 Pi i/5], Sqrt[1 - c2] Sin[4 Pi i/5], \
Sqrt[c2]}, {i, 0, 4}];
frame = Function[{a, b}, {a, b, Cross[a, b]}];
sf = {frame[vecs[[1]], vecs[[2]]], frame[vecs[[3]], vecs[[2]]], \
frame[vecs[[3]], vecs[[4]]],
      frame[vecs[[5]], vecs[[4]]], frame[vecs[[5]], vecs[[1]]]};
Ts = Table[sf[[k + 1]] . Transpose[sf[[k]]], {k, 4}];
logs = MatrixLog /@ N[Ts];
axisOf = Function[m, {m[[3, 2]], m[[1, 3]], m[[2, 1]]}];
gens = axisOf /@ logs;
comms = Flatten[Table[axisOf[logs[[i]] . logs[[j]] - logs[[j]] . logs[[i]]], \
{i, 4}, {j, i + 1, 4}], 1];
{MatrixRank[gens, Tolerance -> 10^-8], MatrixRank[Join[gens, comms], \
Tolerance -> 10^-8]}\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[747]:=",ExpressionUUID->"655d7b11-9ded-2149-a5d7-c13a3eecbccb"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9930477060039444`*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[756]=",ExpressionUUID->"69a8eeb4-d534-3348-b1ea-e571af5b847b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
{2, 3}: the pentagon's own sequence of measurements explores only a \
two-dimensional slice of the rotation group, but one extra step of \
composition closes it into the full three-dimensional group \[LongDash] a \
fixed number set by the single building block, not by how many copies get \
composed. This is the '2 to 3 anchor'. The same fact is prettier in space: \
one generator alone traces a circle (a one-dimensional leaf); the whole \
cascade fills the sphere.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"aeec4e28-b83f-c34a-abdd-996c275f0c8f"],

Cell["\<\
One motion traces a circle (DLA 1); the full cascade fills the sphere (DLA 3) \
\[LongDash] drag the step count:\
\>", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"15ad0b89-2646-ae47-841b-f228e799a0ea"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["c2 = Cos[Pi/5]/(1 + Cos[Pi/5]);\nvecs = Table[{Sqrt[1 - c2] Cos[4 Pi i/5], Sqrt[1 - c2] Sin[4 Pi i/5], Sqrt[c2]}, {i, 0, 4}];\nframe = Function[{a, b}, {a, b, Cross[a, b]}];\nsf = {frame[vecs[[1]], vecs[[2]]], frame[vecs[[3]], vecs[[2]]], frame[vecs[[3]], vecs[[4]]],\n      frame[vecs[[5]], vecs[[4]]], frame[vecs[[5]], vecs[[1]]]};\nlogs = MatrixLog /@ N[Table[sf[[k + 1]] . Transpose[sf[[k]]], {k, 4}]];\naxisOf = Function[m, {m[[3, 2]], m[[1, 3]], m[[2, 1]]}];\ngensV = N[axisOf /@ logs];\nseedV = N[Normalize[{0.3, 0.6, 0.74}]];\nleafV = N[Table[RotationMatrix[t, Normalize[gensV[[1]]]] . seedV, {t, 0, 2 Pi, 2 Pi/160}]];\n(* baked in via With, not referenced as outer globals, so the Manipulate is self-contained *)\nWith[{gens = gensV, seed = seedV, leaf = leafV},\n Manipulate[\n  Module[{walk},\n   SeedRandom[42];\n   walk = NestList[RotationMatrix[0.5, Normalize[gens[[RandomInteger[{1, 4}]]]]] . # &, seed, steps];\n   Graphics3D[{\n     {Opacity[0.10], RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Sphere[{0, 0, 0}, 1]},\n     {RGBColor[.85, .2, .2], Tube[leaf, 0.012]},\n     {GrayLevel[0.3], PointSize[0.011], Point[walk]}\n     }, Boxed -> False, PlotRange -> 1.2, ImageSize -> 330,\n    PlotLabel -> \"red leaf: 1 motion (DLA 1)   •   grey cloud: full cascade (DLA 3)\"]],\n  {{steps, 300, \"cascade steps\"}, 20, 1500, 20, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},\n  ControlPlacement -> Bottom]]", "Input", CellOpen -> False, CellLabel -> "In[757]:=", ExpressionUUID -> "0f958bbe-c6e9-46b1-a8f8-e09a9e0011f8"],

