Cell["\<\
So the wiring pattern is itself a physical design choice with a measurable, \
sometimes decisive, effect \[LongDash] block-counting alone is not enough. \
Note also what did not happen here: this whole composition law lives in graph \
combinatorics, and the geometric Lie-Poisson lens of Stage 5 is applied \
separately, to the mesh's physical realization. By the project's own result \
the two do not track each other. That leaves one uncomfortable question: a \
computer can only test patterns that repeat, so how can 'cct' be called best \
against the infinitely many patterns that never repeat?\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7c791767-2ab9-f843-9437-d22ea3c3bda3"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Stage 8 \[LongDash] Proving cct Is Best: Ergodic Optimization", \
"Section",ExpressionUUID->"4094cb92-12e1-c249-a4f0-0594bc6ea371"],

Cell["\<\
A computer search can only check patterns that repeat. But there are \
infinitely many non-repeating gluing words too, and you cannot brute-force \
them. The fix is to change what you optimize over. The physical quantity that \
actually matters \[LongDash] the mesh's advantage per pentagon as it grows \
without bound \[LongDash] is by definition a long-run average, and the \
precise name for that is the Birkhoff (ergodic) average of a local potential \
\[Phi] that scores the gap contributed by a small window around each junction:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7716d8fc-c685-8a49-858c-0c13515e47b5"],

Cell["    gap(w)  =  lim (1/n) \[Sum] \[Phi](\[Sigma]^k w),   k = 0 .. n-1 ", \
"Text",ExpressionUUID->"dfba0eb5-18b3-014e-998b-cf782dd89d90"],

Cell["\<\
The Birkhoff theorem then converts an intractable search over individual \
infinite words into a search over shift-invariant probability measures \
\[LongDash] a compact, convex space that ergodic-optimization theory (Bousch, \
Ma\[NTilde]\[EAcute], Jenkinson) is built to handle. A periodic word like \
(cct) repeated forever is just one easy candidate, with a natural measure and \
a computable average; the honest open question (hypothesis H1) is whether \
some non-periodic invariant measure could beat every periodic one, a known \
kind of pathology in this field. The repo's tooling \[LongDash] de Bruijn \
graphs, max-plus eigenvalues, policy iteration \[LongDash] produces a ladder \
of certified upper bounds \[CapitalGamma]_k that squeeze the optimum from \
above.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fb4b238b-14ef-3143-9fca-ab1da440bc74"],

Cell["The certificate ladder \[CapitalGamma]_k closing on gap(cct) from \
above:", "CodeText",ExpressionUUID->"0d9502b5-08c9-da45-ae13-b6e887153e9f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
gammaData = {{7, 0.07702057}, {8, 0.07526640}, {9, 0.07202651}, {10, \
0.07145754}};
gapcct = 0.0698975;
Show[
 ListLinePlot[gammaData, PlotMarkers -> Automatic, PlotStyle -> RGBColor[.85, \
.45, .2],
  PlotRange -> {{6.5, 10.5}, {0.068, 0.079}},
  AxesLabel -> {\"certificate depth k\", \"upper bound \[CapitalGamma]_k\"}, \
ImageSize -> 470],
 Graphics[{
   {RGBColor[.2, .45, .85, 0.12], Rectangle[{6.5, gapcct}, {10.5, \
0.07202651}]},
   {RGBColor[.2, .45, .85], Dashed, Line[{{6.5, gapcct}, {10.5, gapcct}}]},
   Text[Style[\"gap(cct) = 0.0698975  (achieved lower bound)\", 8, \
RGBColor[.2, .45, .85]], {8.7, 0.0692}],
   Text[Style[\"open window H1 : width \[TildeEqual] 0.00156\", 8], {8.7, \
0.0710}]}],
 PlotLabel -> \"The certificate ladder squeezes the optimum from above\"]\
\>", "Input",
 CellOpen->False,
 CellLabel->
  "In[775]:=",ExpressionUUID->"21ace2b6-17de-d447-a2fd-a823faa7ee7c"],

